 the most part that's been put to the  side and many of the questions have been  answered  steven beardsley thanks so much  now tesla is just one example of the big  foreign companies who flock to europe's  biggest economy in recent years take a  look at some of the others  this battery factory opened its doors in  2021 and scalefia in northern sweden  it is the first of its kind to be built  by a european company  the swedish company northvolt may only  have been in business since 2016  but it plans to build a battery factory  in northern germany thanks to the  region's eco-friendly power production  and the company can expect substantial  government help  germany needs new batteries to power its  pivot towards green energy  there are also big plans for macdeburg  in eastern germany  u.s chip manufacturer intel wants to  spend 17 billion euros in the region to  open two new factories that would employ  3 000 people  and its banking on european union  subsidies to the tune of several billion  euros  construction is due to start next year  both announcements are likely to give  germany's economy a major boost  in the last several years the amount of  direct investment in germany has been  continually increasing  raising some 10 percent over the last  five years  with the majority of it coming from the  united states  carmaker ford has been working in  germany since 1926  now it's shifting its production to  electric cars  and spending two billion dollars to do  it  at this factory near cologne two new  models will hit the roads starting in  2024.  i'm joined now by robert tehrmann from  germany trade and invest which is the  government's economic promotion  agency robert thanks so much for being  here so germany has been criticized for  a long time for being too slow too  bureaucratic to attract you know foreign  investment is that narrative starting to  change  um of course i think um we have seen  that through the settlement of tesla's  or the decision of tesla to 